+- **Evidence**:
+  - DOI: 10.1038/s41598-024-66110-0 — Classifies FEP vs. healthy controls using PSD features (delta/theta/alpha/low-beta bands) from 30-second segments of 5-minute resting-state EEG. Achieves 95.51% accuracy with Gaussian Process Classifier using balanced borderline-SMOTE resampling. Directly uses the openneuro_ds003944 dataset.
+  - DOI: 10.3390/biomedicines11123223 — Uses ciSSA (complex interval singular spectrum analysis) decomposition with entropy (permutation, spectral), frequency domain, and statistical features extracted from 2-second windows. Achieves AUC=0.9893, accuracy=96.23% with SVM/ANN/ensemble classifiers on the same dataset. Confirms both 30-second and shorter segment approaches are viable.
+- **Kernel Notes**: Resting-state EEG contains no discrete task events; use segmentation-based epoching only. Load `participants.tsv` and extract the group/diagnosis column for each subject. Apply subject-level binary label (FEP=1, Control=0) uniformly to all segments from that subject. For a 5-minute recording at 1000 Hz with 30-second segments and 50% overlap: ~9-10 segments per subject. Extract PSD features per segment (delta 0.5-4 Hz, theta 4-8 Hz, alpha 8-12 Hz, low-beta 12-16 Hz) and train binary classifier. Class imbalance between groups should be checked in participants.tsv before model training. Exclude `unknown` admin events entirely—they contain no usable markers.### Classical Task 2: First Episode Psychosis vs. Control Classification — FEP
